  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half its value as a surface with contours. It is 3 times steeper across (x), so overshoot starts there: rates above 2/3 make the x swings grow.",
    "Each step = −learning rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian with sd 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) per direction: 1.5 at batch 1, exactly 0 at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "The orange arrow is the mini-batch step the ball takes next. The grey arrow is the noise-free full-data step. Both are drawn 3× their true length.",
    "A run ends at 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6 (diverged). A diverged ball is drawn at the bowl's edge, but the loss readout keeps its true value.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed sequence. Reset replays it from the start, and each new run uses the next values. A setting changed mid-run applies from the next step."
  ]
}